1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,216
This video starts slides 9 a: dataflow analysis of straight-line code. Straight-line code has no branches and no loops.

2
00:00:09,666 --> 00:00:19,181
Part 1 optimizes code by intuition and converts it to three-address code. Part 2 computes the same results with equations.

3
00:00:19,631 --> 00:00:30,639
Here is the question type of this part, from questions post 8.1. You get a few lines of code, and you optimize them by intuition, in four steps.

4
00:00:31,089 --> 00:00:37,169
Step 1: rewrite the code in three-address code. Then each statement does one operation.

5
00:00:37,619 --> 00:00:45,320
Step 2: common subexpression elimination. An expression that the code computes twice is computed only once.

6
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:52,319
Step 3: copy propagation. A later use of a copy takes the original variable instead.

7
00:00:52,769 --> 00:00:58,316
Step 4: dead store elimination. An assignment that nothing uses is deleted.

8
00:00:58,766 --> 00:01:10,265
The question bank calls step 4 dead code elimination. The slides call it dead store elimination. It is the same step. First, the background from the slides.

9
00:01:10,715 --> 00:01:18,693
The slides teach dataflow analysis as a learning progression: the same idea, on harder and harder programs.

10
00:01:19,143 --> 00:01:33,821
First, programs in F, the functional language of the course. F has no re-assignment, so a name always holds the same value. There, common subexpression elimination needs no dataflow analysis.

11
00:01:34,271 --> 00:01:43,145
Re-assignment is what creates the need. When a variable can change, the same expression can give a different value later in the code.

12
00:01:43,595 --> 00:01:51,041
Next, C-ish programs. They have re-assignment. First we optimize them by intuition. That is this part.

13
00:01:51,491 --> 00:01:59,213
Then we use equations, on programs without loops. We solve the equations by substitution. That is part 2.

14
00:01:59,663 --> 00:02:13,039
Last, programs with loops. There, we solve by iteration to a fixed point, the technique you started in Lab 4. The new step is to choose clever initial values. That comes after this video.

15
00:02:13,489 --> 00:02:20,273
Two words we use all the time: optimization and dataflow analysis. They are different things.

16
00:02:20,723 --> 00:02:25,630
An optimization transforms the program. It does not need much math.

17
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:33,867
A dataflow analysis does not change the program. It only provides information, and it uses a lot of math.

18
00:02:34,317 --> 00:02:43,085
An analysis informs an optimization. For example, available expressions informs common subexpression elimination.

19
00:02:43,535 --> 00:02:57,956
Dataflow analysis also serves other compiler tasks. The Java Language Specification requires every variable to be definitely assigned before use. Checking that takes a dataflow analysis.

20
00:02:58,406 --> 00:03:04,678
So the rule is: each optimization is informed by at least one dataflow analysis.

21
00:03:05,128 --> 00:03:15,624
In math, optimization has an exact definition: you know when a result is better. The only wrinkle is a local optimum versus the global optimum.

22
00:03:16,074 --> 00:03:21,749
Compilers are an empirical engineering discipline: we manipulate real machines.

23
00:03:22,199 --> 00:03:28,129
What we do to a program might not make it faster on every machine. The slide asks why.

24
00:03:28,579 --> 00:03:37,241
The course notes give the reason. An optimization usually trades one resource for another, for example space to save time.

25
00:03:37,691 --> 00:03:46,075
The memory hierarchy decides how expensive that space is. So the same change can help on one machine and hurt on another.

26
00:03:46,525 --> 00:03:56,765
Some optimizations are even inverses of other optimizations. So in a compiler, optimization means improvement, not a proven optimum.

27
00:03:57,215 --> 00:04:08,265
Here is the secret of dataflow analysis in this course. Almost any question small enough for an exam can be done in your head, without equations, by intuition.

28
00:04:08,715 --> 00:04:16,331
The good news: you then almost always know what answer your equations should produce. Use that answer to check them.

29
00:04:16,781 --> 00:04:22,243
So we start with optimization by intuition. The equations come in part 2.

30
00:04:22,693 --> 00:04:35,941
Common subexpression elimination, C S E, removes work that the code does twice. Here is Example 1 from slide 6. Take x equals 2, y equals 3 and z equals 4.

31
00:04:36,391 --> 00:04:43,196
Line 1 computes x plus y. The values 2 and 3 travel into the addition, and give 5.

32
00:04:43,646 --> 00:04:54,547
Line 2 computes x plus y again. Nothing changed x or y in between, so the result is 5 again. The code does the same work twice.

33
00:04:54,997 --> 00:05:00,693
So compute x plus y once, and keep the value in a new temporary variable, t.

34
00:05:01,143 --> 00:05:07,223
Line 1 of the original becomes a equals t plus z, because t holds x plus y.

35
00:05:07,673 --> 00:05:15,439
Line 2 becomes b equals t times z. Now x plus y is computed one time, not two times.

36
00:05:15,889 --> 00:05:21,073
Check with the values. Both versions give a equals 9 and b equals 20.

37
00:05:21,523 --> 00:05:31,443
The rule: when an expression is computed again, and no operand changed in between, compute it once into a temporary, and use the temporary.

38
00:05:31,893 --> 00:05:40,298
Example 2, from slide 7. Line 2 now has y plus x, not x plus y. Pause the video and try it.

39
00:05:40,748 --> 00:05:47,255
Line 1 computes x plus y. With x equals 2 and y equals 3, that is 5.

40
00:05:47,705 --> 00:05:53,699
Line 2 computes y plus x. Plus is commutative, so this is also 5.

41
00:05:54,149 --> 00:06:01,403
So sort the operands: write y plus x as x plus y. Now the two expressions match exactly.

42
00:06:01,853 --> 00:06:06,482
Then it is Example 1 again. Compute x plus y once, into t.

43
00:06:06,932 --> 00:06:12,372
Both lines use t: a equals t plus z, and b equals t times z.

44
00:06:12,822 --> 00:06:20,161
The rule: sort the operands of a commutative operator, such as plus, before you compare two expressions.

45
00:06:20,611 --> 00:06:27,565
Example 3, from slide 8. Here line 2 assigns 7 to x. Pause the video and try it.

46
00:06:28,015 --> 00:06:34,522
Line 1 computes x plus y. With x equals 2 and y equals 3, that is 5.

47
00:06:34,972 --> 00:06:41,415
Line 2 assigns 7 to x. The value of x changes in place, from 2 to 7.

48
00:06:41,865 --> 00:06:48,179
Line 3 computes x plus y with the new x. 7 plus 3 is 10, not 5.

49
00:06:48,629 --> 00:06:57,952
The two expressions look the same, but their values differ. So C S E is not applicable. Line 3 must compute x plus y again.

50
00:06:58,402 --> 00:07:03,245
Available expressions analysis will detect this. Part 2 shows how.

51
00:07:03,695 --> 00:07:10,564
The rule: an expression is common only if no operand is re-assigned between the two computations.

52
00:07:11,014 --> 00:07:21,745
Example 4, from slide 9. Both lines compute x plus y, then times z. The code does 4 operations. Pause the video and try it.

53
00:07:22,195 --> 00:07:26,547
One round of C S E finds x plus y in both lines.

54
00:07:26,997 --> 00:07:29,983
It computes x plus y once, into t.

55
00:07:30,433 --> 00:07:38,796
Both lines use t: a equals t times z, and b equals t times z. Now the code does 3 operations.

56
00:07:39,246 --> 00:07:45,369
After this round, both lines compute t times z. That is a new common subexpression.

57
00:07:45,819 --> 00:08:01,499
A second round of C S E is needed to find it. Our second round computes t times z once, into u. Then a and b both take u. Now only 2 operations remain, and all versions give 20 for a and for b.

58
00:08:01,949 --> 00:08:07,047
The rule: run C S E again until no expression is computed twice.

59
00:08:07,497 --> 00:08:18,356
Loop invariant code motion, L I C M, works on loops. Here is Example 1 from slide 10. The loop body, line 2, computes x plus y.

60
00:08:18,806 --> 00:08:25,953
Run the loop with x equals 2 and y equals 3. In iteration 0, x plus y is 5.

61
00:08:26,403 --> 00:08:29,923
In iteration 1, x plus y is 5 again.

62
00:08:30,373 --> 00:08:39,909
In iteration 2, it is 5 again. The loop assigns only i and a of i, never x or y. So the value cannot change.

63
00:08:40,359 --> 00:08:48,529
An expression with the same value in every iteration is loop invariant. So compute it once, before the loop, into t.

64
00:08:48,979 --> 00:08:55,017
The loop stays, and a of i takes t. The addition now runs one time, not n times.

65
00:08:55,467 --> 00:08:59,819
The rule: a loop-invariant computation moves before the loop.

66
00:09:00,269 --> 00:09:10,743
Example 2, from slide 11. The body computes b equals x plus y, then a of i equals b times b. Pause the video and try it.

67
00:09:11,193 --> 00:09:18,191
With x equals 2 and y equals 3, b is 5 in every iteration, as in Example 1.

68
00:09:18,641 --> 00:09:28,731
b times b uses only b, and b is 5 every time. So b times b is 25 every time: it is loop invariant too.

69
00:09:29,181 --> 00:09:32,296
Move x plus y before the loop, into t.

70
00:09:32,746 --> 00:09:40,426
Then move b times b before the loop. b holds the value of t, so it becomes u equals t times t.

71
00:09:40,876 --> 00:09:43,969
The loop body only stores u into a of i.

72
00:09:44,419 --> 00:09:53,571
This rewrite removes the assignment to b. That is correct only if b is not used after the loop. The slide states this assumption.

73
00:09:54,021 --> 00:10:00,208
The rule: a computation whose operands are all loop invariant is loop invariant too.

74
00:10:00,658 --> 00:10:09,149
Example 3, from slide 12. The body computes b equals x plus y times i. Pause the video and try it.

75
00:10:09,599 --> 00:10:14,335
In iteration 0, b is 2 plus 3 times 0, which is 2.

76
00:10:14,785 --> 00:10:19,307
In iteration 1, b is 2 plus 3 times 1, which is 5.

77
00:10:19,757 --> 00:10:24,408
In iteration 2, b is 8. So b varies with each iteration.

78
00:10:24,858 --> 00:10:34,031
b uses i, and the loop assigns i in plus plus i. So b is not loop invariant, and L I C M is not applicable.

79
00:10:34,481 --> 00:10:40,199
Reaching definitions analysis can detect this. Part 2 teaches that analysis.

80
00:10:40,649 --> 00:10:46,665
The rule: an expression that uses a variable assigned in the loop is not loop invariant.

81
00:10:47,115 --> 00:10:58,741
Example 3 cannot be hoisted, but it can be made cheaper. The values of b are 2, 5 and 8. Each value is the value before it plus y, which is 3.

82
00:10:59,191 --> 00:11:10,797
So start with b equals x, and add y at the end of each iteration. The multiplication y times i becomes an addition carried from one iteration to the next.

83
00:11:11,247 --> 00:11:14,937
This is called strength reduction. It is not in this course.

84
00:11:15,387 --> 00:11:27,419
C S E needs two clean-up passes: copy propagation and dead store elimination. Real compilers run many passes, and keep each pass as simple as possible.

85
00:11:27,869 --> 00:11:34,568
Here is the code of Example 1 in three-address form. Line 3 computes x plus y again.

86
00:11:35,018 --> 00:11:41,183
Before we optimize, we must know what is used after this snippet. Pause the video and think about it.

87
00:11:41,633 --> 00:11:48,353
a and b are used afterwards: they are live-out. x, y and z come in from before: they are live-in.

88
00:11:48,803 --> 00:11:56,014
C S E: x plus y on line 3 is already in t, so line 3 becomes u equals t.

89
00:11:56,464 --> 00:12:09,200
Line 3 is now a copy: it assigns one variable to another. Copy propagation lets line 4 use t instead of u, because neither u nor t changes in between.

90
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:17,415
Now nothing uses u. Line 4 uses t, and u is not live-out. So u equals t is a dead store.

91
00:12:17,865 --> 00:12:23,369
Dead store elimination deletes it. The result computes x plus y only once.

92
00:12:23,819 --> 00:12:34,187
The rule: C S E leaves copies, copy propagation uses the original instead, and dead store elimination deletes the unused copy.

93
00:12:34,637 --> 00:12:44,152
Programmers sometimes like to write complex expressions. This Java method computes the positive root of the quadratic formula in one line.

94
00:12:44,602 --> 00:12:52,581
Line 2 does nine operations: five multiplications, an addition, a subtraction, a square root and a division.

95
00:12:53,031 --> 00:13:03,377
Compiler engineers want to break such an expression into simple statements, one operation each. That form is easier to analyze and to optimize.

96
00:13:03,827 --> 00:13:10,398
As with desugaring, we work with a form that is convenient for machines, and less so for people.

97
00:13:10,848 --> 00:13:22,261
Three-address code: each statement has at most three variables, so it does at most one operation. Here is the method on b equals x plus y, times z.

98
00:13:22,711 --> 00:13:29,901
Step 1: construct the abstract syntax tree. The parentheses put x plus y below the times node.

99
00:13:30,351 --> 00:13:43,279
Step 2: visit the tree in post-order, bottom-up: the children before their parent. The first internal node we finish is the plus node. It gets number 1 and a new temporary, t.

100
00:13:43,729 --> 00:13:47,953
The plus node becomes the statement t equals x plus y.

101
00:13:48,403 --> 00:13:58,621
Next comes the root, the times node. Its children are now t and z. The root assigns the original variable: b equals t times z.

102
00:13:59,071 --> 00:14:09,205
The rule: build the A S T, visit it in post-order, and give each internal node a new temporary. The root assigns the original variable.

103
00:14:09,655 --> 00:14:16,695
Now the quadratic formula. Pause the video and try it: build the tree, and name the internal nodes.

104
00:14:17,145 --> 00:14:31,225
The tree follows Java precedence: times and divide bind tighter than plus and minus. Equal operators group from left to right. So 4 times a times c is 4 times a, then times c.

105
00:14:31,675 --> 00:14:38,928
Post-order starts at the far left. The first finished node is minus 1 times b. Name it neg B.

106
00:14:39,378 --> 00:14:43,495
Next, inside the square root: b times b becomes b 2.

107
00:14:43,945 --> 00:14:46,335
Then 4 times a becomes four A.

108
00:14:46,785 --> 00:14:49,281
Then four A times c becomes four A C.

109
00:14:49,731 --> 00:14:55,405
The minus node takes b 2 and four A C: disc equals b 2 minus four A C.

110
00:14:55,855 --> 00:15:00,890
The square root node: sqrt equals Math dot sqrt of disc.

111
00:15:01,340 --> 00:15:05,287
The plus node: num equals neg B plus sqrt.

112
00:15:05,737 --> 00:15:09,406
The right subtree of the root: den equals 2 times a.

113
00:15:09,856 --> 00:15:16,619
Last, the root: q equals num divided by den. Nine internal nodes give nine statements.

114
00:15:17,069 --> 00:15:24,301
Slide 18 writes the result as a Java method, with longer names. Each line does one operation.

115
00:15:24,751 --> 00:15:34,628
Lines 4 and 5 split 4 times a times c. Java groups it as 4 times a, then times c, as the tree on the right shows.

116
00:15:35,078 --> 00:15:43,185
One complex return statement became eight simple statements and a return of numerator divided by denominator.

117
00:15:43,635 --> 00:15:51,720
Now the whole pipeline of Example 1 in one picture. The original two lines become four lines of three-address code.

118
00:15:52,170 --> 00:15:55,413
C S E changes line 3 to u equals t.

119
00:15:55,863 --> 00:16:00,321
Copy propagation changes line 4 to b equals t times z.

120
00:16:00,771 --> 00:16:05,443
Dead store elimination deletes u equals t. Three lines remain.

121
00:16:05,893 --> 00:16:16,475
Each optimization is informed by a dataflow analysis. C S E uses available expressions. It works forward, and it is a must analysis.

122
00:16:16,925 --> 00:16:24,626
Copy propagation uses an analysis with the same name, copy propagation. It is also forward and must.

123
00:16:25,076 --> 00:16:34,399
Dead store elimination uses reaching definitions. It is forward, and it is a may analysis. Part 2 explains must and may.

124
00:16:34,849 --> 00:16:50,977
Slides 20 to 22 give the reason for each step. C S E: the expression x plus y is available on line 3. Neither x nor y has been re-assigned since x plus y was last computed, on line 1.

125
00:16:51,427 --> 00:17:00,899
Copy propagation: the copy u equals t from line 3 propagates to line 4. Neither u nor t is re-assigned on the way.

126
00:17:01,349 --> 00:17:08,730
Dead store elimination: u equals t is a dead store. This definition of u is not used anywhere.

127
00:17:09,180 --> 00:17:18,631
Each reason is a fact about the lines between two points of the program. A dataflow analysis computes such facts. Part 2 shows how.

128
00:17:19,081 --> 00:17:23,646
Here is the method card for this question type. It has five steps.

129
00:17:24,096 --> 00:17:34,869
Step 1: write the three-address code. Build the A S T with Java precedence, visit it in post-order, and give each internal node a new temporary.

130
00:17:35,319 --> 00:17:49,463
Step 2: C S E. When an expression is computed again, and no operand was re-assigned in between, use the temporary of the first computation. Sort the operands of plus and times first.

131
00:17:49,913 --> 00:18:00,153
Step 3: copy propagation. After a copy u equals t, a later use of u takes t, as long as neither u nor t is re-assigned.

132
00:18:00,603 --> 00:18:08,582
Step 4: dead store elimination. Delete an assignment whose variable is not used later and is not live-out.

133
00:18:09,032 --> 00:18:12,893
Step 5: repeat steps 2 to 4 until nothing changes.

134
00:18:13,343 --> 00:18:24,074
Question 8.1.1 of questions post. Optimize these two lines by intuition. a and b are used afterwards. Pause the video and try it.

135
00:18:24,524 --> 00:18:33,228
Step 1: three-address code. In line 1, the post-order visit finishes x plus y first: t equals x plus y.

136
00:18:33,678 --> 00:18:39,907
Then k plus n becomes u equals k plus n. The root gives a equals t plus u.

137
00:18:40,357 --> 00:18:50,043
Line 2 the same way, with new temporaries: v equals x plus y, w equals k plus n, and b equals v times w.

138
00:18:50,493 --> 00:19:01,266
Step 2: C S E. x plus y on line 4 is already in t, and neither x nor y changed. So line 4 becomes v equals t.

139
00:19:01,716 --> 00:19:08,116
k plus n on line 5 is already in u. So line 5 becomes w equals u.

140
00:19:08,566 --> 00:19:21,174
Step 3: copy propagation. Line 6 uses v and w. v is a copy of t, and w is a copy of u. So line 6 becomes b equals t times u.

141
00:19:21,624 --> 00:19:30,691
Step 4: dead store elimination. Now nothing uses v or w, and they are not live-out. Delete lines 4 and 5.

142
00:19:31,141 --> 00:19:36,175
Step 5: repeat. No expression is computed twice now, so we stop.

143
00:19:36,625 --> 00:19:40,849
The answer has four lines. It matches the posted solution.

144
00:19:41,299 --> 00:19:45,246
Question 8.1.2. Pause the video and try it.

145
00:19:45,696 --> 00:19:58,560
Step 1: three-address code. Times binds tighter than plus. So in line 1, y times z is computed first: t equals y times z, then a equals x plus t.

146
00:19:59,010 --> 00:20:07,053
In line 2, z times x is computed first: u equals z times x, then b equals u plus y.

147
00:20:07,503 --> 00:20:18,276
Step 2: C S E. The four expressions are y times z, x plus t, z times x, and u plus y. No expression is computed twice.

148
00:20:18,726 --> 00:20:27,003
So steps 3 and 4 have nothing to do, and the code cannot be optimized. The question tests operator precedence.

149
00:20:27,453 --> 00:20:31,443
Question 8.1.3. Pause the video and try it.

150
00:20:31,893 --> 00:20:44,415
Step 1: three-address code. Java computes y times z first. Then the plus and minus operators group from left to right: x plus t, then plus k, then minus n.

151
00:20:44,865 --> 00:20:55,148
Line 2 the same way: h equals y times z, i equals x minus h, j equals i minus n, and b equals j plus k.

152
00:20:55,598 --> 00:21:03,598
Step 2: C S E. y times z on line 5 is already in t. So line 5 becomes h equals t.

153
00:21:04,048 --> 00:21:14,331
The other expressions differ: x plus t is not x minus h, and u plus k is not i minus n. So only y times z is common.

154
00:21:14,781 --> 00:21:24,125
Step 3: copy propagation. Line 6 uses h, a copy of t. So line 6 becomes i equals x minus t.

155
00:21:24,575 --> 00:21:30,356
Step 4: dead store elimination. Nothing uses h now. Delete line 5.

156
00:21:30,806 --> 00:21:41,153
Step 5: repeat. Nothing else is common. This is our solution. Java's left-to-right order keeps every value the same as in the original code.

157
00:21:41,603 --> 00:21:50,733
Recap of part 1. An analysis provides information. An optimization changes the program, and an analysis informs it.

158
00:21:51,183 --> 00:22:00,485
C S E computes an expression once, if no operand is re-assigned in between. Sort the operands of plus and times first.

159
00:22:00,935 --> 00:22:07,484
L I C M moves a computation with the same value in every iteration before the loop.

160
00:22:07,934 --> 00:22:15,529
Three-address code: build the A S T, visit it in post-order, and give each internal node a new temporary.

161
00:22:15,979 --> 00:22:22,976
Copy propagation and dead store elimination clean up after C S E. Live-out variables stay.

162
00:22:23,426 --> 00:22:32,535
Part 2 computes the facts behind these steps with equations: available expressions, copy propagation, and reaching definitions.
